course: dictionary
lesson: 13868
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one fits. Read the sentence around the word and ask: what makes sense here? «The soldiers talked about the Kaiser, the ruler of Germany.» The sentence is about a country and its ruler, so Kaiser means an emperor.
explain: «Posters of the time often made fun of the Kaiser.» Made fun of tells you that people were talking about him and laughing at him, so the Kaiser must be a famous person. Look for clues like this in the words that come before and after.
explain: «Mum bought six kaisers at the bakery.» The bakery is the clue. A kaiser here is a round, crusty bread roll, not an emperor. The same word can mean two very different things, so always check the other words in the sentence.
fact: Kaiser is a title, and it means emperor.
fact: «Kaiser» means the emperor of Germany from 1871 to 1918.
fact: «The Kaiser» usually means Kaiser Wilhelm II, the German emperor in the First World War.
fact: «Kaiser» comes from the Roman name «Caesar», like the Russian «tsar».
fact: In the US, a «kaiser roll» is a round, crusty bread roll.
exercise: «The Kaiser ruled Germany during the First World War.» What does the Kaiser mean here? | A Roman soldier | A bread roll | *The emperor of Germany
exercise: «Dad bought a kaiser roll for my sandwich.» What is a kaiser roll? | *A round, crusty bread roll | A small wooden boat | A kind of cake with cream
exercise: The word kaiser comes from a Roman name. Which name is it? | *Caesar | Roman | Tsar | Wilhelm
test: «The Kaiser fought against Britain and France.» Who is the Kaiser in this sentence? | A Roman general | A type of sandwich | *The ruler of Germany in the First World War | A famous British king
test: «Six kaisers were cooling on the bakery shelf.» What are the kaisers here? | Emperors of Germany | Old cartoons | Soldiers in a war | *Round, crusty bread rolls
exam: Which sentence uses kaiser to mean an emperor? | «Grandma baked kaisers for the picnic.» | *«The Kaiser ruled Germany during the First World War.» | «Kaisers are sold in every bakery.» | «The kaiser was warm and crusty.»
